.page ping6 1 send ICMPv6 echo requests to a host

# SYNOPSIS
*ping6* [*-c* _COUNT_] [*-i* _SECONDS_] [*-W* _SECONDS_] [*-s* _BYTES_] [*-t* _HOPS_] [*-q*] _HOST_

# DESCRIPTION
`ping6` is `ping -6`: it sends an ICMPv6 echo request to _HOST_ (a name that has an AAAA record, or an IPv6 address) once a second and prints a line for every answer: the size of the reply, the sender, the sequence number and the round-trip time. Without `-c` it goes on until interrupted with Ctrl+C. At the end it prints how many packets were sent and received and the shortest, average and longest round trip.

It uses an ICMPv6 datagram socket, so any user may run it. The kernel numbers the requests and checks their checksum; the first request to a neighbour waits a moment while the neighbour's hardware address is looked up (neighbour discovery, `ip -6 neigh` shows the result).

A link-local address (`fe80::...`) is only meaningful on one cable, so it is written with the interface after a percent sign: `fe80::2%eth0`. When there is exactly one card that is up the `%eth0` may be left out; with several cards the request fails with `Invalid argument` and the card has to be named.

If a router or the stack itself answers with an error the line says so (`Destination unreachable: No route`, `Packet too big`, `Time exceeded: Hop limit`, `Parameter problem`) instead of a reply. A request that gets no answer within the timeout prints `Request timeout for icmp_seq N`. A request bigger than the path allows (the size is up to 65000 bytes) is sent in fragments and the reply is put together again.

# OPTIONS
@ *-c* _COUNT_
    Stop after COUNT requests.
@ *-i* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long between requests (at least 0.2, default 1).
@ *-W* _SECONDS_
    Wait this long for each answer (default 2).
@ *-s* _BYTES_
    Size of the data in the request (8 to 65000, default 56).
@ *-t* _HOPS_
    Hop limit of the outgoing packets (the IPv6 name for the time to live).
@ *-q*
    Quiet: only the summary.

# EXAMPLES
    ping6 -c 3 ::1
    ping6 -c 3 fec0::2
    ping6 -c 1 fe80::2%eth0
    ping6 -c 1 -s 3000 ::1

# EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one answer arrived, 1 if none did, 2 for a usage error or a name that does not resolve.

# SEE ALSO
ping(1), ip(1), nslookup(1), network(7)
